+# LLD Review: MOB-25109/offline-content-card-availability
+
+## Summary
+
+Two non-functional stubs (network gate always returns true; `inMemoryContentCardPropositions` written but never read), one documentation/implementation contract inversion on the feature flag default, one known but unresolved completion handler correctness bug, and several medium-severity design inconsistencies in the new offline CC state management layer.
+
+---
+
+## Design Issues
+
+### CRITICAL: `isNetworkAvailable()` is a hardcoded stub — network gating is entirely non-functional
+
+**Files:**
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:297-301`
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:412-416` (guard in `handleProcessEvent`)
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:1035-1041` (guard in `fetchPropositions`)
+
+**Problem:**
+```swift
+private func isNetworkAvailable() -> Bool {
+    // TODO: wire up to a real reachability signal ...
+    // Hardcoded `true` for now so the network gate never blocks update/fetch requests.
+    return true
+}
+```
+The PR introduces two `guard isNetworkAvailable() else { ... }` call sites — one in `handleProcessEvent` for `updatePropositions` events, one in `fetchPropositions`. Both are dead guards. They never fire. The network-availability gating that the feature is named after does not exist.
+
+**Why it matters:** The offline CC strategy depends on distinguishing "network available" from "network unavailable" to decide whether to short-circuit an Edge request and serve from disk. Shipping a stub that always returns `true` means no short-circuit ever happens. The disk-read paths (`usePersistedContentCards`, boot hydration) still work, but the automatic fallback to disk when offline — implied by the feature name — is not wired up. Callers who call `updatePropositionsForSurfaces` on a device with no network will wait for an Edge timeout rather than failing fast.
+
+**Extensibility blast radius:** Adding real network reachability tomorrow touches:
+- `Messaging.swift` — replace stub body (1 file)
+- Potentially an injection point if the function needs to be mockable in tests (1 more file)
+
+VERDICT: 2 files need changes = GOOD EXTENSIBILITY once the stub is replaced, but the current design ships a feature that does not function.
+
+**Fix:**
+```swift
+private func isNetworkAvailable() -> Bool {
+    return SystemInfoService().isNetworkAvailable  // or MobileCore.isNetworkAvailable() once exposed
+}
+```
+If the real signal is not yet available in AEPCore, this guard block should not be merged: the guards are misleading dead code, not a safe no-op forward stub.
+
+**Confidence:** 1.0 — the implementation is literally `return true`.
+
+**Caveats:** None. The code is explicit.
+
+**Challenge:** One could argue the disk-serve paths work fine without it and the stub is a forward scaffold. Counter: scaffolds that silently disable feature-critical logic are worse than not having the scaffold — they pass tests, give no observable signal, and mislead reviewers.
+
+---
+
+### CRITICAL: `inMemoryContentCardPropositions` is dead state — written on every network response, never read
+
+**Files:**
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:158-161` (declaration + misleading doc comment)
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging+State.swift:67-88` (`updateContentCardPropositions` — only write site)
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:707` (`clearContentCards` — only clear site)
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:1386` (doc comment: "this function never reads from `inMemoryContentCardPropositions`")
+
+**Problem:**
+The variable declaration states:
+> "Engine hydration reads from here rather than re-reading disk on every call."
+
+This is false. Every hydration path explicitly bypasses this variable:
+- `hydrateContentCardRulesEngineFromDisk` reads `cache.contentCardPropositions` directly.
+- `hydrateContentCardPropositionsRulesEngine` doc comment explicitly says "this function never reads from `inMemoryContentCardPropositions`."
+- `retrieveMessages` reads from `qualifiedContentCardsBySurface` (already evaluated), not from raw propositions.
+- The commented-out optimization at line 1286 (`// if !inMemoryContentCardPropositions.isEmpty {`) is the only place it was intended to be read, but is disabled.
+
+Every live network CC response executes two dictionary writes and one origin-map update into this variable for zero observable effect.
+
+**Why it matters:**
+1. Memory: every CC ruleset proposition from every live fetch is accumulated in this dictionary for the lifetime of the session with no consumption. On a device with many surfaces, this is unbounded growth.
+2. Correctness risk: the false doc comment will cause future maintainers to believe this dict is the source of truth for network CC propositions. Code that reads it expecting live network data would silently return stale or empty data.
+3. Abstraction clarity: the distinction between `inMemoryContentCardPropositions` (raw, never evaluated) and `qualifiedContentCardsBySurface` (evaluated, the actual read path) is not represented in the type system — it is only in comments, and those comments are currently wrong.
+
+**Fix:**
+Either complete the intended optimization (use `inMemoryContentCardPropositions` as the source for re-hydration so boot-path disk reads are skipped on subsequent `getContentCardsUI` calls), or remove the variable entirely. Do not ship dead state with a false documentation contract.
+
+If keeping for future use, mark it explicitly: rename to `_pendingNetworkContentCardPropositions` and add a `// TODO(PLATIR-XXXXX): not yet consumed` comment rather than a doc comment claiming it IS consumed.
+
+**Confidence:** 1.0 — confirmed by exhaustive grep across all source files.
+
+**Caveats:** Test files may read it; did not audit test helpers.
+
+**Challenge:** Could argue this is forward scaffolding for a future optimization. Counter: the doc comment actively misrepresents the current behavior, which is worse than no comment.
+
+---
+
+### HIGH: `isContentCardOfflineAvailable` defaults to `false` but comment documents it as "defaults to `true`"
+
+**Files:**
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:303-311`
+
+**Problem:**
+```swift
+/// Defaults to `true` when the key is absent (backwards-compatible: offline availability is on
+/// unless the operator explicitly disables it). Pass `event: nil` to read the latest state.
+private func isContentCardOfflineAvailable(for event: Event? = nil) -> Bool {
+    let config = getSharedState(extensionName: ..., event: event)
+    let value = config?.value?[MessagingConstants.SharedState.Configuration.CONTENT_CARD_OFFLINE_AVAILABLE] as? Bool ?? false
+    ...
+    return value
+}
+```
+
+The documented behavior is `?? true`; the implementation is `?? false`. When the `messaging.contentCardOfflineAvailable` key is absent from the remote configuration (which is the default state for any operator that has not explicitly opted in), the feature is disabled — CC propositions are NOT persisted to disk, and `usePersistedContentCards: true` calls return empty.
+
+**Why it matters:** The AGENTS.md and PR description both describe this feature as automatically available. The contract inversion means:
+- Boot-time disk hydration still runs (it's unconditional) — but there is nothing on disk to hydrate because no writes ever happened.
+- `updateContentCardPropositions` disk write is skipped for every session until the operator configures the key.
+- `usePersistedContentCards: true` in `retrieveMessages` will return empty arrays and log "contentCardOfflineAvailable is false — returning empty."
+
+Operators testing the feature without the config key will observe silent non-function with no error.
+
+**Fix:**
+```swift
+let value = config?.value?[MessagingConstants.SharedState.Configuration.CONTENT_CARD_OFFLINE_AVAILABLE] as? Bool ?? true
+```
+Or, if the intent is `?? false`, update the doc comment to match and document the operator opt-in requirement clearly.
+
+**Confidence:** 1.0 — the mismatch is literal.
+
+**Caveats:** The intent may actually be `false` (opt-in model), in which case the doc comment is the bug. Either way one of them is wrong.
+
+**Challenge:** Could argue `false` default is intentional (explicit operator opt-in). Counter: the doc comment says the opposite, and the AGENTS.md summary table does not describe an opt-in gate.
+
+---
+
+### HIGH: Completion handler unconditionally reports `true` even after non-recoverable Edge errors
+
+**Files:**
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:1222-1247` (`endRequestFor`)
+
+**Problem:**
+```swift
+private func endRequestFor(eventId: String) {
+    // KNOWN ISSUE: ... `handler.handle?(true)` ... still unconditionally reports success.
+    // A caller ... cannot currently distinguish this case from a genuine success.
+    applyPropositionChangeFor(eventId: eventId)
+    requestedSurfacesForEventId.removeValue(forKey: eventId)
+    nonRecoverableErrorEventIds.remove(eventId)   // clears the failure record
+    ...
+    if let handler = completionHandlerFor(edgeRequestEventId: UUID(uuidString: eventId)) {
+        handler.handle?(true)                      // always true
+    }
+}
+```
+
+The code's own comment identifies this as a known bug and even prescribes the fix (`capture nonRecoverableErrorEventIds.contains(eventId) before it's cleared`). The information needed to pass `false` is available inside `applyPropositionChangeFor` via `requestFailed`, but it is not surfaced back to `endRequestFor`.
+
+**Why it matters:** Any caller using `updatePropositionsForSurfacesWithCompletionHandler` to decide whether to fall back to `usePersistedContentCards: true` (a primary use case for this feature) cannot distinguish a real success from a failed network request. The completion handler is the only signal available to callers.
+
+**Fix:** The comment already has the fix. Capture the flag before clearing:
+```swift
+let requestFailed = nonRecoverableErrorEventIds.contains(eventId)
+applyPropositionChangeFor(eventId: eventId)
+requestedSurfacesForEventId.removeValue(forKey: eventId)
+nonRecoverableErrorEventIds.remove(eventId)
+inProgressPropositions.removeAll()
+if let handler = completionHandlerFor(edgeRequestEventId: UUID(uuidString: eventId)) {
+    handler.handle?(!requestFailed)
+}
+```
+
+**Confidence:** 1.0 — the bug is self-documented in the code.
+
+**Caveats:** Changing `handler.handle?(true)` to `handler.handle?(!requestFailed)` is a behavior change that could break callers relying on the current always-success contract. Requires a targeted call site audit.
+
+**Challenge:** The code comment says this is known and deferred. Counter: deferring a known-wrong completion handler in the same PR that introduces the completion-handler-driven fallback pattern defeats the purpose of the pattern.
+
