+
+### HIGH: `clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()` in disabled-feature branch ignores `requestFailed` — can wipe valid state on network failure
+
+**Files:**
+- `AEPMessaging/Sources/Messaging.swift:1281-1290` (inside `applyPropositionChangeFor`)
+
+**Problem:**
+```swift
+let requestFailed = nonRecoverableErrorEventIds.contains(eventId)
+// ...
+let surfacesToRemove = requestFailed ? [] : requestedSurfaces.minus(returnedSurfaces)
+
+if ccOfflineAvailable {
+    ccWriteOK = cache.updateContentCardPropositions(...)
+} else {
+    clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()   // ← runs regardless of requestFailed
+    ccWriteOK = true
+}
+```
+
+When `ccOfflineAvailable = false` AND `requestFailed = true`:
+- `surfacesToRemove` is correctly set to `[]` (don't evict on failure).
+- `clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()` runs unconditionally, wiping `qualifiedContentCardsBySurface`, `contentCardRulesBySurface`, `contentCardOriginByProposition`, `inMemoryContentCardPropositions`, and the CC rules engine.
+
+The carefully computed `requestFailed` guard is entirely bypassed in the disabled-feature branch. A non-recoverable Edge error on a device where the config key is absent (or explicitly false) will silently destroy any previously qualified CC state.
+
+**Why it matters:** Even with the feature disabled, CC cards are still qualified in memory via live network responses. Destroying them on a network error, when the intent is clearly "preserve on failure," is a logic inconsistency that will be hard to diagnose in production (no observable error, cards just disappear).
+
+**Fix:**
+```swift
+} else {
+    if !requestFailed {
+        clearPersistedContentCardPropositions()
+    }
+    ccWriteOK = true
+}
+```
+
+**Confidence:** 0.95 — the `requestFailed` bypass is clear. A small residual uncertainty: whether the intent was truly to always-clear when the feature is disabled (treating disabled as "never cache" regardless of request outcome).
+
+**Caveats:** If the design intent is "feature disabled = always-clear on every response regardless of failure", that intent should be documented explicitly and the `requestFailed` variable should be named differently to signal it doesn't apply here.
+
+**Challenge:** Could argue "feature disabled means never maintain CC state at all." Counter: `requestFailed` is specifically designed to preserve state when network fails, and that intent should apply whether or not the feature flag is on.
+
